I've hit the practical limit of what I can retrieve this session: WebSearch was exhausted after my very first batch of queries (the session's 200-query budget was already spent before this task), so all further research had to go through WebFetch against guessed/known URLs — which succeeded for well-established Wikipedia pages and failed (404/403) for ACM DL, specific research-lab pages, and several niche sites. I was not able to complete the 30+ search sweep the task called for. Below is what I actually verified live this session, clearly separated from gaps I could not fill and from background knowledge I'm not able to re-verify right now.

## What I verified live this session (WebFetch, cited)

**Estonia ROCA/Infineon crisis (high confidence, verified):** ~750,000 Estonian ID/e-residency cards issued Oct 2014–Oct 2017 were vulnerable to a flaw letting private keys be inferred from public keys. Certificates were suspended Nov 2–3, 2017; renewal window ran through March 31, 2018; unrenewed certs were revoked April 1, 2018. Card manufacturer Gemalto later paid €2.2M compensation (Feb 2021) over failing to timely disclose. This is a real single-root mass-revocation event with a measured remediation window (~5 months) — a concrete data point for "what does recovery from a compromised root actually cost in wall-clock time." Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estonian_identity_card

**X-Road Estonia–Finland federation (high confidence on mechanism, no failure data found):** Connected Feb 7, 2018. Architecture is peer-to-peer — each organization holds its own signing/auth keypairs, no central intermediary brokers trust. This is a genuinely federated two-region model, but Wikipedia documents no operational difficulties; I could not source a measured account of what was actually hard about it. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Road — **gap: no measured friction data found.**

**Aadhaar scale and failure modes (medium-high confidence):** ~1.383B enrolled (Oct 2024), >99.9% of adult population. UIDAI itself reported (2018) a 12% biometric authentication error rate. Andhra Pradesh/Telangana's 2015 Aadhaar–voter-ID linkage disenfranchised ~3 million voters, later cancelled by the Supreme Court. The Sept 2018 Supreme Court judgment upheld the Aadhaar Act's constitutionality but barred mandatory use for bank accounts, mobile SIMs, and school admissions. I could not retrieve the Reetika Khera/Jean Drèze ration-denial-death studies or Jharkhand-specific numbers directly this session (404s on targeted fetches) — that material is real and well-known from training but I could not re-verify or get exact figures live. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aadhaar. **Gap: exclusion-death studies not independently re-verified this session.**

**IPV tech-abuse research corpus (high confidence — this is the strongest hit of the session):** Fetching ipvtechresearch.org returned a real, current bibliography confirming and extending your target list:
- Freed et al., "A Stalker's Paradise: How Intimate Partner Abusers Exploit Technology," CHI 2018, Best Paper Award.
- Freed et al., "Is my phone hacked? Analyzing Clinical Computer Security Interventions with Survivors of IPV," CSCW 2019, Best Paper Honorable Mention.
- Havron et al., "Clinical Computer Security for Victims of Intimate Partner Violence," USENIX Security 2019 (founding paper of the "clinical computer security" model — treating device compromise like a medical intake).
- Chatterjee et al., "The Spyware Used in Intimate Partner Violence," IEEE S&P (Oakland) 2018.
- Tseng et al., "The Tools and Tactics Used in Intimate Partner Surveillance," USENIX Security 2020, Distinguished Paper.
- Roundy et al., "The Many Kinds of Creepware Used for Interpersonal Attacks," IEEE Oakland 2020.
- Daffalla et al., "Account Security Interfaces: Important, Unintuitive, and Untrustworthy," USENIX Security 2023, Distinguished Paper — this is squarely on-point for your identity-model question: it measures how account-recovery UX itself creates an attack surface for a co-resident abuser.
- Daffalla et al., "A Framework for Abusability Analysis: The Case of Passkeys in Interpersonal Threat Models," USENIX Security 2025 — directly relevant if your ladder touches passkey-style device-bound credentials.
- Bellini et al., "Shortchanged: … Intimate Partner Financial Abuse," CHI 2024; "Paying the Price," CHI 2023 Best Paper; "The Digital-Safety Risks of Financial Technologies for Survivors of IPV," USENIX Security 2023.
- Bellini et al., "SoK: Safer Digital-Safety Research Involving At-Risk Users," IEEE Oakland 2024.
Source: ipvtechresearch.org. I could not fetch individual abstracts (404s on guessed permalink paths), so I have titles/venues/awards confirmed live but not the numeric findings inside each paper — **treat the specific numbers from this cluster as training-knowledge, medium confidence, not re-verified this session.**

**Decidim (medium confidence):** 311 instances across Spain + 19 countries (2022), ~400 governments/institutions by Decidim's own count. Barcelona's 2017 pilot: ~40,000 participants, ~11,000 proposals, ~8,000 accepted. Three-tier user model (visitor / registered / verified) confirmed, but the actual residency-verification mechanism behind "verified" was not in the fetched page — **gap: census-verification method not confirmed live.** Documented failure modes: in Lucerne's top-down deployment the platform "strengthened the digital divide rather than overcoming it"; in Badalona, participation stopped when the sponsoring administration changed after an election — i.e., civic participation infrastructure measured as politically non-durable, not technically fragile. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decidim

**NYC Mesh (medium confidence):** >2,000 active member nodes across NYC boroughs, incorporated nonprofit 2019, 501(c)(3) 2020, a 2026 grant of $898,035 to hire its first paid staff — implying the network ran purely on volunteer labor up to that point. No explicit burnout data surfaced. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NYC_Mesh

**Freifunk (medium-high confidence):** ~400 independent local communities, ~41,000 access points in Germany, coordinated loosely via the Picopeering Agreement (no central membership register). The dominant *measured* failure at community-mesh scale here isn't identity at all — it's legal: German *Störerhaftung* (operator secondary liability) suppressed public hotspot deployment for years until ECJ/German Federal Court rulings 2016–2018 narrowed it; Freifunk's workaround was VPN-tunneling all traffic through Sweden. This is a genuinely different failure class than anything in the household or Aadhaar rungs — a governance/liability problem, not an authentication or account-sharing problem. Source: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freifunk

**Sardex (low-medium confidence, thin data):** Founded 2010, Sardinia. Annual participation fee €200–3,000 by company size; new members get unsecured mutual credit up to a capacity-based limit; system has "occasional problems of needing to sue participants to recover debt." No scale/turnover numbers or academic-outcome citations were retrievable from the fetched page — **gap: the well-known Sardex econometric studies (Sardex as counter-cyclical liquidity during the Eurozone crisis) exist in the literature but I could not source them live this session.**

**Guifi.net, MitID, ICAO PKD detail, Pan-Canadian Trust Framework, Reetika Khera/Drèze primary sources, Swedish BankID fraud statistics, EUDI Large-Scale Pilot findings, the cryptographers' Article 45 open letter: all returned 404/no-data on the URLs I could construct, and WebSearch was unavailable for the rest of the session to find the correct URLs.** These are real, well-documented items I have background familiarity with, but I am not able to certify current figures without live verification, and presenting them as newly-confirmed would misrepresent what this session actually checked.

## Assessment against your hypothesis

Even on this partial pull, one pattern is visible and worth flagging at high confidence: **the failure modes are categorically different at each rung, not just larger in scale.**
- Household rung: failures are *relational/coercive* — the account-recovery flow, the shared device, the "who has the passcode" question become an attack surface between people who trust each other in every sense except this one (Daffalla et al. 2023/2025, Havron et al. 2019 "clinical" framing exists precisely because no ordinary security model anticipates the adversary living in the house).
- Community rung: failures are *legal/governance* (Störerhaftung) and *political durability* (Decidim's Badalona case), not identity-technical.
- Region rung: failures are *infrastructural-root* (Estonia's 750K-card revocation) and *statistical-exclusion-at-scale* (Aadhaar's 12% biometric error rate translating into real exclusion).
- Cross-region: the only clean example I could verify (X-Road) is deliberately peer-to-peer with no common root, but I could not find measured friction data for it — suggesting this seam is under-measured in the literature relative to the others, which is itself a finding worth noting as a negative result.

This is consistent with — though not sufficient on its own to prove — your hypothesis that the household rung is hardest: it's the only rung where the literature treats the adversary and the trusted party as the same person, which no purely technical identity model (however well it handles scale) is designed to survive.

**Given the WebSearch outage, I'd recommend re-running the remaining items (Guifi.net compact critique, Sardex econometrics, MitID complaint data, Swedish BankID fraud numbers, EUDI pilot results, the Article 45 open letter, Reetika Khera primary sources) once the search budget resets — I can pick this back up.**
